  "metadata": {
    "overall_location": "a medieval tavern",
    "starting_location": "the tavern's wooden floor",
    "setting_description": "The Stone Hearth Tavern is a rectangular stone hall measuring eighty feet east‑west and sixty feet north‑south, with thick stone walls that creak under the wooden floor. The south wall hosts the main entrance: a single back door centered on the wall, its wooden latch protruding from the rear to allow inward swing. A clear aisle at least twelve feet wide runs from this doorway straight north toward the hall’s northern edge.\n\nAlong the east side of the hall sits the counter—a three‑foot‑wide bar that stretches twenty feet in length. Its centreline lies ten feet north of the south wall and fifteen feet west of the eastern wall, leaving ample space for patrons to move around. The polished tables are set along this counter; each is four by four feet and fixed at their positions. Near the back end of the counter a high bar stool stands beside a shelf of crystal cups that rises six feet tall and three feet deep.\n\nOn the west wall sits the hearth, occupying a ten‑by‑twelve‑foot stone area centred fifteen feet northward from the south wall. Beside this hearth an upright barrel—eight feet high with a four‑foot diameter—is positioned on the floor. The rear northwest corner of the hall holds a single back table measuring five by five feet; it is placed three feet north‑west of the counter’s centreline, ensuring no overlap with other furniture or walls.\n\nAbove each polished table and above the back table hangs a lantern suspended six feet from the floor, providing illumination for patrons. All objects are fixed in place except the wooden latch on the entrance door, which can be moved to open or close the hall. The layout preserves the medieval tavern’s authentic spatial arrangement with stone walls, creaking floors, and ample room for movement around the counter and hearth.",
    "spatial_location_description": "The tavern occupies a rectangular stone hall that measures **80 ft** from east to west and **60 ft** from north to south.\n\n- **Entrance:** The main door is the wooden back‑door on the **south wall**, centered at its middle. A latch protrudes from the back of this door, allowing it to swing inward toward the hall.\n  \n- **Counter (bar):** Runs along the **east side** of the hall, 3 ft wide and 20 ft long. It is positioned so that its centreline lies 10 ft north of the south wall and 15 ft west of the east wall. The counter holds several polished tables—each table measures 4 ft by 4 ft—and a high bar stool (2.5 ft tall) sits near the back end of the counter, adjacent to a shelf of crystal cups that is 6 ft tall and 3 ft deep.\n\n- **Hearth:** Located on the **west wall**, the hearth occupies a 10‑by‑12‑foot stone area centred at y = –15 (northward from the south wall). Beside this hearth, a barrel sits upright; it is 8 ft high with an average diameter of 4 ft.\n\n- **Back table:** Occupying the rear northwest corner of the hall. This single table measures 5 ft by 5 ft and stands 3 ft north‑west from the centre of the counter, ensuring no overlap with other furniture or walls.\n\n- **Lanterns:** A lantern hangs above each table—both those on the counter and the back table—for illumination. Each lantern is suspended 6 ft above the floor level at its respective table’s position.\n\nAll objects are placed so that doors remain accessible; the south‑wall entrance opens onto a clear aisle of at least 12 ft wide, running from the door to the north wall. The east and west walls provide ample space for patrons to move around the counter and hearth without obstruction. No furniture overlaps any stone wall or other piece of furniture, preserving the medieval tavern’s authentic spatial layout."
  },
